.venv
.mypy_cache
.pytest_cache
.ruff_cache
__pycache__
*.py[cod]
# The same at-any-depth rule the `.env` comment below spells out, applied to the two trees that
# are large rather than secret. `.claude/worktrees` holds a full checkout per parallel session,
# each with its own `server/.venv`, and a bare `.venv` pattern matches only the context root --
# so on 2026-09-08 a deploy shipped 3.25GB of other sessions' virtualenvs to the daemon, filled
# its disk mid-build, and took the running api down with it. Only `.claude/worktrees` is
# excluded, never `.claude` itself: `.claude/settings.json` is tracked, and excluding a tracked
# file reads as a deletion to the builder's identity check.
.claude/worktrees
**/.venv
**/node_modules
# Every .env, at any depth -- not just the one at the root. A bare `.env` pattern matches only
# the context root, so `client/.env` was copied in and Vite baked the platform API key into the
# built bundle inside the image. A secret in an image travels further than a secret on a page.
.env
**/.env
**/.env.*
!**/.env.example
workspaces
*.db
.coverage
coverage.xml
htmlcov
.claude/settings.local.json
# Build outputs and dependency trees. Both are gitignored, so excluding them cannot upset the
# builder's checkout-identity check, and copying a client node_modules into the context costs
# hundreds of megabytes for files the image rebuilds from the lockfile anyway.
node_modules
client/dist

# Nothing tracked may be excluded here. The builder stage verifies checkout identity with
# `git status --porcelain` inside the context, so an excluded tracked file reads as a
# deletion and fails the build. Runtime exclusion belongs in the builder's `rm -rf`, which
# already drops `.codex-prompts` from the image.
